    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d154">

                <pb facs="0357" n="237" xml:id="pg237"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15A4/1179: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Chairman of the American Delegation (Gibson)
                        to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Geneva</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1932-06-25T15:00:00+01:00">June 25, 1932—3
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received June 25—12:05 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>277. Wilson and I have had two conversations with Matsudaira and Sato in the last
                    few days. They fear that their Government will feel that the worst reduction of
                    fleets bears more heavily on the weaker than on the stronger powers in spite of
                    the unchanged mathematical ratio.</p>
                <p>They had been under the misapprehension that the President’s proposal covered the
                    abolition of land bombardment aviation only and were greatly relieved and
                    interested to learn from us that the prohibition on air bombardment and the
                    abolition of bombers applied equally to naval forces.</p>
                <p>You may feel it wise to apprise the Ambassador in Tokio of this misapprehension
                    as it may be mentioned to him.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gibson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
